## Why This Is Asked

The problem mirrors real-world build systems and DAG validations, requiring both cycle detection and a valid execution order.

## Key Concepts

- DFS with 3-color marking to detect cycles
- Topological sort via postorder push
- Handling multiple components in a possibly disconnected graph

## Code Example

```javascript
function dfsTopo(graph) {
  const n = graph.length;
  const color = Array(n).fill(0); // 0 white, 1 gray, 2 black
  const order = [];
  const cycles = [];
  function dfs(u) {
    color[u] = 1;
    for (const v of graph[u] || []) {
      if (color[v] === 0) {
        if (dfs(v)) return true;
      } else if (color[v] === 1) {
        cycles.push([u, v]);
        return true;
      }
    }
    color[u] = 2;
    order.push(u);
    return false;
  }
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    if (color[i] === 0) {
      if (dfs(i)) break;
    }
  }
  return { topo: order.reverse(), cycle: cycles.length ? cycles : null };
}
```

